/* Source held under a name, and placed where you say.

   `[ ... ]` holds code the program around it is not reading yet, and the tag
   says who reads it. `c-language` means a C compiler will; `cufet` means this
   language will. One rule decides what happens to either: SAYS WHAT IT GIVES
   BACK, so you run it — SAYS NOTHING, so it is source, and `Cite` places it.

   What a block places goes where that kind of declaration belongs. A type
   belongs to the whole program wherever it is written. A function belongs
   where functions live. A value belongs to the block it is written in — so a
   cited value lands at the site that cited it, and the same block cited twice
   makes two of them. None of that is a rule this feature adds; it is what the
   language already said about declarations, applied to a declaration you
   chose the position of.

   A block may only reach for names that belong to the PROGRAM — a function, a
   type, a `permanently` constant, a pulled book. It can never quietly pick up
   a local from wherever you happened to cite it, which is what makes citing
   the same block in two places mean the same thing both times. */

Pull a book on cufet.

    /* Says nothing about a result, so it is SOURCE. Three declarations that
       belong together: the type, the constant it is measured with, and the
       helper that does the measuring. A function can hand back a value —
       another function among them — but it cannot hand back a TYPE, and it
       cannot leave three names behind for the code after it to use. */
    Define cufet reading-vocabulary as [
        Define object reading with (the text subject, the number letters, the number words):
            Bind text to shown:
                Return "{one's words} words, {one's letters} letters: {one's subject}".
            Done.
        Done.

        Define the word-break as " " permanently.

        Bind number to letters-in, given (the text line):
            Define the total as 0.
            For each word in the line split by the word-break, repeat:
                The total becomes the total + the length of word.
            Done.
            Return the total.
        Done.
    ].

    /* One line places all three. The type is now the program's, the helper is
       an ordinary function, and the constant is a constant here. A block that
       holds a function may only be cited where functions belong — at the top
       level, or directly inside a `Pull` block like this one — because placed
       inside another body it would close over that body, and a block must
       mean the same thing wherever it is put. */
    Cite reading-vocabulary.

    /* Says what it gives back, so it is something you RUN. The body is a
       body: it holds a loop, its own locals, and reads the constant the block
       placed. This is the same shape a c-language axiom has, and the same
       rule chose it — only the language inside the brackets differs. */
    Define cufet number words-in, given (the text line), as [
        Define the count as 0.
        For each word in the line split by the word-break, repeat:
            The count becomes the count + 1.
        Done.
        Return the count.
    ].

    /* Nothing crosses a boundary here, so anything Cufet has can come back —
       a `series of text` among it. A c-language axiom is held to a number, a
       fact or a voidable text, because those are what survive the crossing;
       there is no crossing to survive. */
    Define cufet series of text words-of, given (the text line), as [
        Return the line split by the word-break.
    ].

    Define the line as "the quick brown fox".

    Define the note as a new reading {
        the subject the line,
        the letters cast letters-in on (the line),
        the words cast words-in on (the line)
    }.
    State cast shown on (the note).

    For each word in cast words-of on (the line), repeat:
        State "  {word} ({the length of word})".
    Done.

    /* And the part that is only true of values. Both functions cite one
       block, and each gets its own tally — write the set-up once, place it
       where it is needed. A function could have handed back a 0; it could not
       have left the name `the tally` behind for the next line to use. */
    Define cufet fresh-tally as [
        Define the tally as 0.
    ].

    Bind number to counted-up:
        Cite fresh-tally.
        The tally becomes the tally + 5.
        Return the tally.
    Done.

    Bind number to left-alone:
        Cite fresh-tally.
        Return the tally.
    Done.

    State "counted up: {cast counted-up on ()}".
    State "left alone: {cast left-alone on ()}".
Done.
